# ai.wing — Structural Changelog
# Blue Ridge Communications · Independent Authorized Retailer Landing Site
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Format: [BUILD] / [ADD] / [CHANGE] / [FIX] / [DATA] / [QA]
# Every structural change, feature addition and component creation is logged
# here for version tracking.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


## v1.0.0 — Initial build

### [BUILD] Project foundation
- Next.js 15.5.4 · App Router · TypeScript (strict) · React 19.
- Tailwind CSS v4 via @tailwindcss/postcss, CSS-first `@theme` configuration.
- Manual scaffold: `create-next-app` refused the directory name
  ("Blue Ridge Communications" — spaces and capitals violate npm naming),
  so package.json / tsconfig / next.config / postcss.config were authored
  directly. Package name set to `blue-ridge-authorized-retailer`.
- Dependencies: framer-motion ^12, lenis ^1.3 (animation only — no backend,
  no API routes, no database; this is a strict frontend build).
- `.claude/launch.json` added with `autoPort: true` for local preview.

### [DATA] Brand palette scraped from https://www.brctv.com
Hex values extracted from the live computed stylesheet, ranked by usage:
- #0866FF  electric   — primary action blue (most-used accent on brctv.com)
- #002F8C  navy       — deep corporate blue
- #1E398D  royal      — mid royal blue on panels
- #021562  abyss      — darkest navy (base for the luxury dark surfaces)
- #01A7E1  cyan       — bright sky accent
- #B2ECFF  sky        — pale cyan tint
- #F2FCFF  ice        — near-white icy background wash
- #414042  ink        — charcoal body text
- #F25648  coral      — warm contrast accent (used only for error states)
Derived tokens `--color-br-void` / `--color-br-pitch` extend the navy ramp
downward for the deep-space hero and dark sections.

Typography: brctv.com sets Avenir Next LT Pro (licensed, not redistributable).
Substituted Plus Jakarta Sans for display and Inter for body — the closest
geometric-humanist pairing available through next/font/google, self-hosted at
build time so there is no render-blocking font request.

### [DATA] Service audit of https://www.brctv.com
Pages audited: /, /internet, /fiber-internet, /streaming-and-tv, /tv,
/mobile, /home-phone, /internet-mobile-brtp-offers, /internet/homefi,
/standard-pricing, /disclosure.

Services Blue Ridge sells natively, and therefore built:
- Fiber .......... Blue Ridge Fiber, 100% fiber-to-the-home (no public rate card)
- Cable .......... 7 published speed tiers, 300 Mbps to 2 Gig
- Bundles ........ 3-year internet + free mobile line offers
- TV ............. Blue Ridge Stream, Stream+ Live TV, 5 Live TV packages
- Mobile ......... Free Unlimited Line, Unlimited $30/line, Shared Data $15/line
- Phone .......... Blue Ridge Home Phone (no public rate card)

Blue Ridge also sells Smart Home Security. It is outside the six service
lines specified for this page and was deliberately not added.

### [ADD] lib/content.ts — single source of truth
Every price, speed, feature bullet, FAQ, fine-print row, legal page and nav
item on the site is read from this one file. No component hard-codes copy or
pricing. Exports:
- `PlanItem` / `ServiceSection` / `FeatureItem` / `FaqItem` / `LegalPage` types
- `site` — retailer identity, sales phone, disclosure string, service area
- `hero` — headline, subhead, live promos, stat rail
- `marqueeItems` — trust ticker content
- `serviceSections` — the six service lines in their rendered order
- `streamOptions` — the two Blue Ridge Stream platform options
- `finePrint` — inclusions/fees comparison matrix + notes
- `whyUs` — staggered feature grid
- `faqs` — accordion content
- `legalPages` — the 8 legal policies, with full body copy
- `footer` — columns and reseller disclosure
- Helpers: `formatSpeed()`, `ctaLabel()`, `lowestPrice`

### [ADD] Components

Animation primitives
- `SmoothScroll.tsx` ....... Lenis smooth scrolling + anchor interception,
                            disabled entirely under prefers-reduced-motion
- `Reveal.tsx` ............. `useSafeInView` hook, `Reveal`, `TextReveal`
                            (per-word rise + unblur), `StaggerGroup`/`StaggerItem`
- `MagneticButton.tsx` ..... cursor-following CTA with spring damping, a label
                            that drifts further than the shell, and a sheen sweep
- `BlackHoleField.tsx` ..... canvas accretion disk + event horizon + starfield
- `SectionWipe.tsx` ........ `ClipWipe` (clip-path section transitions) and
                            `Parallax` (scroll-linked depth)
- `ConnectivityPath.tsx` ... SVG path-draw of a fiber run reaching a home network
- `Marquee.tsx` ............ CSS-only infinite ticker, pauses on hover

Content components
- `PriceLockup.tsx` ........ THE universal price formatter (see below)
- `PlanCard.tsx` ........... 3D tilt pricing card with specular highlight
- `TopDisclosureBar.tsx` ... persistent retailer disclosure strip
- `Header.tsx` ............. sticky header, scroll-spy nav pill, mobile sheet
- `Hero.tsx` ............... promos, H1, ZIP checker + call button, stat rail
- `ServiceBlock.tsx` ....... renders any `ServiceSection`; three layouts
                            (split / grid / TV ladder) chosen from the data
- `FinePrintGrid.tsx` ...... desktop table + mobile card stack
- `WhyUs.tsx` .............. staggered feature grid with wide-span cards
- `Faq.tsx` ................ accordion with sticky heading column
- `Footer.tsx` ............. dark footer, call band, 8 legal links, disclosure
- `Icons.tsx` .............. inline SVG glyph set (no icon-font payload)

### [ADD] Universal price lockup
`<PriceLockup plan={plan} tone size />` is the only place a price is
formatted anywhere on the site:
- muted "As low as" eyebrow in 0.7rem uppercase
- dollar sign set small and raised
- large integer at 3.5rem (lg) / 2.5rem (md), tabular figures, -0.045em tracking
- cents rendered as muted raised superscript
- "/mo" suffix baseline-aligned to the integer
- promo qualifier beneath, constrained to 26ch
- a plan with no `price` renders the "Pricing by phone" lockup instead of a
  fabricated number

### [ADD] Routes
- `/` .................... the one-page landing site
- `/legal/[slug]` ........ 8 statically generated legal pages
- `/not-found` ........... branded 404

### [ADD] Section hierarchy (fixed order)
1. Top disclosure bar
2. Sticky header with anchor nav + call CTA
3. Hero — live promos, H1, ZIP checker AND call button, black hole background
4. Trust marquee (dual counter-scrolling rails)
5. Fiber
6. Cable
7. Bundles
8. TV
9. Mobile
10. Phone
11. Honest fine-print grid
12. Why Blue Ridge (staggered grid)
13. FAQ accordion
14. Legal footer

Tone alternates dark / light down the page; every dark-to-light handoff is a
scroll-driven clip-path wipe rather than a hard edge. The fine-print-to-
features seam is dark-on-dark, so it uses a luminous hairline instead.

### [FIX] motion() called during render
`Reveal` built its motion component with `motion(as)` inside the render body,
which creates a new component type on every render and remounts the subtree.
Replaced with static `motion.div` / `motion[as]` references.

### [FIX] Reveals stranded at opacity 0 when IntersectionObserver stays silent
Framer Motion's `whileInView` is a thin wrapper over IntersectionObserver,
which never reports in a few real situations — a tab that loads while hidden,
a prerender or screenshot pass, some embedded webviews. Every animated
element would then be stuck at opacity 0 and the page would read as blank.
Replaced `whileInView` with a `useSafeInView` hook that observes normally but
arms a 1.4s fallback at mount; any genuine observer callback disarms it, so
below-the-fold content still animates on scroll. The same guard was added to
`ConnectivityPath`.

### [CHANGE] Black hole tuning
First pass centred the horizon behind the headline at 0.29 of the viewport
minimum, which haloed the type and buried the copy. Moved the horizon to
82% of hero height at 0.17 scale, cut the photon-ring alpha roughly in half,
biased particle density inward and drew the event horizon before the ring so
the disk reads as a horizon line beneath the headline rather than a target
behind it.

### [CHANGE] Headline accent gradient
`text-gradient-brand` ramps across four stops, so applying it per word (the
reveal animation splits the phrase into per-word spans) restarted the ramp on
every word and broke mid-word. Added `text-accent-glow`, a tight two-stop
vertical ramp that reads identically at any word position.

### [QA] Negative constraints verified
- No support routing. Nothing directs a customer to the carrier for billing,
  outages or support.
- Hero carries BOTH the ZIP availability checker AND a prominent call button.
- CTA copy rule enforced through `ctaLabel()`: a plan with a price renders
  "Call to order", a plan without one renders "Call for pricing". Raw phone
  numbers appear as button text only in the navbar and the footer.
- No competitor bashing; no "ordering channel" or similar phrasing.
- No empty placeholder sections. Every service line rendered is one Blue
  Ridge sells natively.
- No links to official Broadband Facts labels.
- No "Pricing observed [date]" stamps anywhere.
- No FAQ asking whether this is the official site, and none explaining
  authorized-retailer legal structure.
- Footer carries the reseller disclosure and all 8 required legal pages.

### [QA] Performance and accessibility
- Production build: 12 static routes, 175 kB First Load JS on `/`.
- Canvas clamps DPR to 1.5, scales particle count to viewport width, and
  suspends its rAF loop via IntersectionObserver and `visibilitychange`.
- Marquee is pure CSS — no main-thread work.
- `prefers-reduced-motion` honoured globally and per component: Lenis does
  not mount, the canvas paints one static frame, tilt and magnetic effects
  are bypassed, and path draws render complete.
- Fonts self-hosted through next/font with `display: swap`.
- Skip link, focus-visible rings, semantic landmarks, `aria-expanded` on the
  accordion and mobile menu, `aria-live` on the ZIP result, table markup with
  scoped headers for the fine-print grid.

### [NOTE] Before launch
`site.phoneDisplay` / `site.phoneHref` in `lib/content.ts` currently hold
(855) 555-0143, from the 555-01xx range reserved for fictional use, so the
build never dials a live third-party line. Replace both with the tracked
sales number and every CTA across the site updates.

### [FIX] Blank render with scripting disabled
Framer Motion serialises its `initial` state into the SSR markup as inline
`opacity: 0`. If the bundle never executes — scripting off, a CDN failure —
the page would render blank. Added a `<noscript>` stylesheet that forces
every element carrying an inline zero opacity back to visible.

### [CHANGE] Plan card price alignment
Prices sat below the "Ideal for" chip list, which wraps to a different number
of lines per tier, so the price lockups landed at different heights across a
row — visible immediately on the seven-tier cable grid. Moved the price
directly under the speed readout, which is a uniform height on every card.
Measured after the change: 0px spread in price position within every row of
the cable, bundle and mobile grids, and 0px spread on CTA position.


## v1.1.0 — Brand alignment pass

### [CHANGE] Site now presents as Blue Ridge Communications
`site.retailerName` changed from "Ridgeline Connect" to "Blue Ridge
Communications", which propagates to the header wordmark, the footer, page
titles and Open Graph metadata through the single source of truth.

The footer disclosure had to be reworded rather than string-replaced: the
original read "<retailer> is an independent authorized retailer of Blue
Ridge Communications", which becomes self-referential once the retailer name
IS Blue Ridge Communications. It now reads "This website is operated by an
independent authorized retailer of Blue Ridge Communications and is not the
corporate website of Blue Ridge Communications."

Disclosure remains prominent in three places: the persistent top bar, the
"Authorized Retailer" line under the header wordmark, and the full footer
disclosure.

### [DATA] Exact section colours pulled from brctv.com
The first pass sampled brand colours from the computed stylesheet. This pass
went further and read the actual section background gradients, which is what
gives the site its colour distribution:

  speeds section    linear-gradient(#FFFFFF 0%, #F2FCFF 50%, #B2ECFF 100%)
  products section  linear-gradient(0deg, #CDF9FD 0%, #FFFFFF 100%)
  hero deal band    linear-gradient(#0077DD 0%, #00368C 99%)
  footer            #1E398D
  flat light fill   #F2FCFF

New tokens: --color-br-azure #0077DD, --color-br-deep #00368C,
--color-br-aqua #CDF9FD. Removed --color-br-pitch and --color-br-void, the
two near-black navies invented for the previous dark-dominant treatment, and
--color-br-electric-soft, now unused.

### [CHANGE] Light-dominant surface system
The site was dark-dominant; brctv.com is light-dominant with vibrant and
deep-navy bands as anchors. Added `components/Surfaces.ts` with four
surfaces — deep, vibrant, light, white — each mapping to one of their
gradients, plus a `dark` flag that drives text treatment below section level.

Distribution down the page:
  hero        surface-hero     (azure to abyss)
  marquee     surface-royal    (#1E398D)
  fiber       surface-light
  cable       surface-white
  bundles     surface-vibrant  (their deal gradient — the offer-led section)
  tv          surface-light
  mobile      surface-white
  phone       surface-light
  fine print  surface-deep
  why us      surface-light
  faq         surface-white
  footer      surface-royal    (#1E398D, matching theirs exactly)

### [ADD] surface-hero
The hero could not use their deal gradient verbatim. That band on brctv.com
carries one line of text; this hero carries a headline, a paragraph, a form
and a stat rail. Starting at #0077DD left white body copy under 4.5:1. The
hero runs the same three brand stops but reaches the deep end faster:
linear-gradient(172deg, #0077DD 0%, #00368C 38%, #021562 100%).

### [CHANGE] Hero backdrop — black hole replaced with RidgeAurora
The black hole was generic and off-brand. Replaced with the company's own
motif: brctv.com uses themes/custom/brctv/images/mtns-lt-blue.svg, two
angular peaks filled #00A7E1 at 6% opacity, as a section background. That
geometry is extended here into four parallax ridge layers with sunlit crest
highlights, a drifting aurora and a sparse starfield.

`RidgeAurora.tsx` is a server component — zero JS shipped. The aurora and
shimmer are CSS keyframes on transform and opacity only, so the backdrop is
GPU-composited, runs no rAF loop and costs nothing on the main thread. The
old canvas implementation ran a per-frame particle simulation.

The same peak geometry is reused as `ridge-motif`, an inline data-URI SVG
applied to the light sections, exactly as Blue Ridge uses it.

### [CHANGE] Hero restructured
- Removed the "Call to order" button. The hero now leads with the ZIP
  availability checker alone; the call CTA remains in the header, in every
  plan card and in the footer.
- Added a featured-offer panel carrying the headline promotion currently
  running on brctv.com — $50/month for up to 500 Mbps over 36 months, a free
  unlimited mobile line for 12 months, a free year of Total Protection, and
  included modem, HomeFi and Stream units — with its own price lockup and
  the AutoPay/Paperless qualifier.
- Two-column layout: copy and ZIP checker left, offer panel right, with the
  three supporting promotions below.

### [REMOVE] "How the connection reaches you"
The entire panel is gone, along with `ConnectivityPath.tsx` and its SVG
path-draw animation and the `.draw-path` / `.draw-dot` CSS. The fiber
section's supporting column is now the "Ideal for" cards alone.

### [CHANGE] All section copy rewritten for a professional register
Conversational and figurative headings replaced throughout — hero H1 and
subhead, all six service lines, the fine-print grid, the features grid, the
FAQ intro and the footer CTA.

