### [FIX] Header contrast over the hero
Unscrolled, the header sits over the lightest stop of the hero gradient,
where the wordmark and white/75 nav links fell below AA. Added a scrim
behind the header — a top-down #021562 fade that is present when transparent
and fades out as the bar condenses — and raised inactive nav links to
white/85. Verified in the DOM that the wordmark renders #FFFFFF and the
links white/85 against the darkened strip.


## v1.2.0 — Photography

### [DATA] Eight images received and processed
All eight arrived as 1408×768 PNG, roughly 2.2MB each (17.5MB total), rather
than the per-slot aspect ratios and JPG format the manifest asked for. Two
problems had to be solved before they could be used.

**Gemini's visible watermark.** Every frame carried a four-point sparkle
roughly 118px in from the right edge and 120px up from the bottom. Each file
was cropped on whichever edge cost its composition least:

  bottom 150px   fiber-installation, cable-work-from-home,
                 tv-family-evening, tv-voice-remote, homefi-shelf,
                 local-technician-porch          -> 1408×618
  right 178px    mobile-main-street (subject's feet sit low in frame),
                 phone-kitchen-counter (notepad runs to the bottom edge)
                                                 -> 1230×768

**Weight.** Converted to WebP at quality 84. 17.5MB of PNG became 727KB —
a 96% reduction. Originals preserved untouched in `public/images/_source/`.

Processing used `sharp`, added as a dev dependency. Next.js also uses sharp
for its own image optimization in production, so it is not extra surface.

### [ADD] Photography wired through the single source of truth
Images are content, so they live in `lib/content.ts` alongside the copy they
illustrate, not scattered through components. Added a `Media` type
(`{ src, alt }`), an optional `media` field on `ServiceSection` and on
`FeatureItem`, and a required `media` on each `streamOptions` entry.

They are statically imported rather than referenced by URL string, which
lets Next.js derive intrinsic dimensions and generate a blur placeholder for
each at build time — that is what prevents layout shift as they load.

Placement:
  fiber-installation      fiber section, heads the supporting column
  cable-work-from-home    cable section, beside the heading
  tv-voice-remote         Blue Ridge Stream card header
  tv-family-evening       Blue Ridge Stream+ Live TV card header
  mobile-main-street      mobile section, beside the heading
  phone-kitchen-counter   home phone section, heads the supporting column
  local-technician-porch  Why Us, "Local customer service" card
  homefi-shelf            Why Us, "HomeFi with eero Pro 7" card

The HomeFi card was promoted to a wide span so its photo has room.

### [ADD] components/SectionMedia.tsx
A rounded photo frame with the treatment that keeps photography sitting on
brand rather than looking pasted in: a navy gradient veil in multiply, a
#0077DD wash in overlay, an inset hairline that works on both light and dark
surfaces, and a slow scale on hover. Grid sections render it beside the
heading in a two-column header; split sections render it above the
"Ideal for" cards.

### [CHANGE] Section layouts adapted for photography
- Grid-layout sections with a `media` field now render heading and photo as
  a two-column header rather than a full-width heading block.
- The two TV platform cards gained edge-to-edge photo headers with a
  gradient fade into the card body.
- Why Us cards render as photo cards when they carry `media`, and as glyph
  cards when they do not.

### [NOTE] Outstanding
- `hero-ridges-dusk` was not delivered. The hero still uses the RidgeAurora
  backdrop, which stands on its own; the photographic layer can be added
  behind it whenever the file arrives.
- `mobile-main-street` has faint garbled lettering on the background
  storefront signage — an AI artifact. It is small, well out of focus and
  sits under the section's gradient veil, but it is the one image that would
  not survive close inspection at full size.


### [ADD] Hero photograph
`hero-ridges-dusk` was in the folder after all — an earlier directory
listing did not show it and it was wrongly reported as missing.

Processed like the rest: cropped bottom 150px to remove the sparkle
watermark (the foreground trees along the bottom edge were the least
valuable part of the frame), then enlarged to 2752px wide with lanczos3
before encoding. The enlargement adds no detail, but the hero is full-bleed
and a 1376px source left the browser upscaling ~2.1x on a 2x display; doing
it here keeps the enlargement clean and stops Next.js stretching to its
3840px candidate. 1.82MB PNG -> 108KB WebP at 2752x1236.

### [CHANGE] RidgeAurora replaced by HeroBackdrop
The drawn SVG ridge layers are gone. Stacking illustrated peaks over
photographed ones read as duplication, so the photograph now carries the
depth on its own. `RidgeAurora.tsx` deleted, `HeroBackdrop.tsx` added.

### [FIX] Colour grade was burying the photograph
The first version of HeroBackdrop ran a multiply layer
(azure/55 -> deep/70 -> abyss/95), an overlay pass, two blooms and a
left scrim at abyss/70 over the image. Combined, they flattened the
photograph into what looked like a plain blue gradient — the ridgelines
were completely invisible, and it read as though the image had failed to
load. Confirmed by zeroing the overlay opacities in the live DOM, which
brought the photograph straight back.

The exposure is already squarely in the brand's blue range, so it does not
need a colour grade at all — only enough shading to carry white type. Both
blend layers were removed. What remains is a flat abyss/30 wash, a
left-weighted scrim for the copy column, a top wash under the header and a
base wash into the next section. The ridgelines now read clearly behind the
offer panel while the headline keeps its contrast.


## v1.3.0 — Navbar, promotion emphasis, layout cleanup

### [FIX] Header rendered against white, not the hero
The header is `sticky`, which keeps it in normal flow, so it sits on the
page background rather than over the hero. Left transparent, it rendered
against the white body — and the scrim added in v1.1 to darken the hero
was in fact darkening white, producing the grey band visible at the top of
the page.

The scrim is gone. The header now carries its own brand surface: solid
#021562 beneath the royal disclosure bar, with a faint azure radial so it
is not a flat block, going frosted on scroll as before.

### [CHANGE] Hero overlay reduced
The grade was still heavier than it needed to be after v1.2. Flat wash
30% -> 12%, left scrim mid-stop 35% -> 18%, blooms trimmed, and the
redundant top wash removed now that the header is opaque. The ridgelines
read clearly across the full width.

### [ADD] Promotion and headline emphasis
- `.promo-ring` — a conic-gradient border sweep around the hero offer
  panel, driven by an `@property` angle so it animates on the compositor.
- `.promo-glow` — a breathing cyan bloom behind the panel, synced to the
  same 7s period.
- `.accent-pulse` — a slow luminance pulse on the headline accent words.
- `.headline-highlight` — a brand accent rule that wipes in beneath the
  headline once the word reveal has settled.
All four are disabled under prefers-reduced-motion.

### [ADD] mediaAsBackground
New optional flag on `ServiceSection`. When set, the section photo renders
full-bleed behind the content instead of as a framed card, and the section
switches to its dark treatment regardless of the surface the page assigned
it. Applied to Home Phone. The split layout suppresses the inline media
card when the flag is set, so the photo is never shown twice.

### [CHANGE] Why Us grid regularised
Photo cards and glyph cards shared one `auto-rows-fr` grid, so every text
card stretched to the height of a photo card and sat mostly empty. Split
into two grids: the two photo cards as an even pair, then the six
remaining commitments on a three-up grid. Measured after the change —
photo row 2x 606x456, glyph rows 3x 397x222 and 3x 397x222, all uniform.

### [CHANGE] Copy
- "Mobile service at straightforward monthly rates"
    -> "Nationwide mobile service, priced per line"
- "What is included, and what is billed separately"
    -> "Inclusions, equipment and billing terms"
  Both supporting paragraphs rewritten to match.

### [CHANGE] Footer copyright
Year removed; now reads "© Blue Ridge Communications. All rights
reserved." The `new Date().getFullYear()` call went with it.


## v1.4.0 — Ambient motion, promotion emphasis, copy density

### [ADD] components/AuroraField.tsx
Three brand-coloured blobs on independent drift paths (30s / 42s / 36s, so
they never sync), heavily blurred so they read as a slow shift of light
rather than discrete shapes. `tone` picks the palette: cyan/azure/sky on
the light surfaces, cyan/electric/royal on the deep ones.

Pure CSS keyframes on transform only, and a server component, so it adds
no client JS and no main-thread work. Now present on all eight sections —
fiber, cable, bundles, tv, mobile, fine print, why us, faq.

It replaced the scroll-linked `Parallax` blobs in those sections. Those
only moved while scrolling; the aurora is always alive, which is what
makes the page feel less static when it is sitting still. `Parallax`
remains exported for the hero's photo depth.

### [ADD] Motion utilities in globals.css
- `.aurora-a/b/c` — the three drift paths
- `text-accent-live` — replaces the static accent ramp on the hero
  headline with the same brand colours panning across 260% background-size
- `.rule-live` — the headline accent rule keeps its gradient panning after
  the wipe-in finishes
- `.sheen-sweep` — a skewed light sweep crossing the offer panel every 6.5s
- `.levitate` — a 10px vertical float on the offer panel
- `.mote` — drifting light motes rising through the hero
All disabled under prefers-reduced-motion.

### [ADD] components/CountUp.tsx
The hero's featured price counts from 0 to 50 on first view. It reserves
its final width with an invisible copy of the finished number, so the
lockup does not reflow while the digits change, and it renders the final
value immediately under prefers-reduced-motion.

### [CHANGE] Hero overlay reduced again
Flat wash 12% -> 6%, left scrim mid-stop 18% -> 10%. The ridgelines,
cloud detail and sky gradient now all read clearly while the headline
keeps its contrast against the left-weighted scrim.

### [CHANGE] Why Us copy density
The section was the densest text block on the page. Description cut from
~230 characters to 102; card bodies from 120-160 characters down to
35-85. Measured after: glyph card height 222px -> 200px, all uniform.
The `span: 'wide'` flags were dropped from the data since the grid now
derives its layout from whether a card carries a photo.

Examples:
  "The people answering the phone live in the same communities the network
   runs through. That is the whole reason the service quality holds up."
    -> "The team answering your call lives in the communities the network
        serves."
  "CableFax Magazine named Blue Ridge its 2025 Independent Operator of the
   Year: small-town values running on genuinely modern infrastructure."
    -> "Named by CableFax Magazine in 2025."

### [QA] Cost of the motion layer
Page JS 18.6 kB -> 22 kB (the CountUp component). Everything else is CSS
on the compositor: no rAF loops, no scroll listeners, no client JS for the
aurora fields.


### [CHANGE] Hero photograph now shown untinted
Removed every colour layer sitting over the hero image: the flat
`bg-br-abyss/6` wash, the three drifting aurora blooms (cyan/electric/
azure), and the `saturate-[1.05]` filter on the image itself. Together
they were pulling the frame toward one flat blue and flattening the
ridgelines.

The left legibility scrim was rebuilt with hard stops rather than a smooth
full-width fade — 0.80 at 0%, 0.62 at 18%, 0.28 at 36%, 0.06 at 48%, fully
transparent by 56% — so the copy column keeps its contrast and the right
44% of the photograph is completely unmodified. Verified in the DOM: one
full-frame layer, image filter `none`.

What is left over the photograph is six 2-3px white motes and a bottom
wash confined to h-40 for the handoff into the next section.


### [FIX] Hero photograph was being upscaled, which is what made it soft
Reported as "clarity is not there". Measured in the browser: the served
bitmap was being scaled up 7.12x to cover the hero box (2.3x even on a
wide desktop). Two causes, both mine.

1. The watermark crop took 150px off the BOTTOM. That was the wrong edge.
   It discarded 20% of the vertical pixels and pushed the aspect out to
   2.23:1, while the hero container is roughly 1.32:1. `object-cover`
   therefore crops the sides and scales to fill the HEIGHT — the exact
   dimension that crop had shrunk. Re-cropped from the untouched
   `_source` PNG trimming the RIGHT edge instead: 1230x768 at 1.60:1,
   full height retained, then enlarged to 2560x1598 with lanczos3 at
   quality 90. 263KB.

2. `sizes="100vw"` was wrong for a cover-fitted image. Cover needs
   containerHeight x sourceAspect pixels of width, which is well beyond
   the viewport width, so Next.js was picking a variant far too small and
   the browser was stretching it. Now declares
   `(min-width: 1280px) 150vw, (min-width: 768px) 200vw, 340vw`.

Added 2560 to `images.deviceSizes` so the candidate set contains an exact
match for the source; without it the step above 2048 is 3840 and the
optimizer would upscale past the real pixel count for nothing.

Measured after: cover scale 7.12x -> 1.20x. Forest texture, autumn colour,
the warm valley lights and the star field are all legible again.


## v1.5.0 — Card iconography

### [ADD] ServiceGlyph — one mark per product family
Six hand-drawn 24px stroke glyphs in the existing icon style, keyed by
`ServiceLine` so any component can render the right mark from plan data
alone:
  fiber   strands converging into a lit connector
  cable   modem with a signal arc
  bundle  stacked package solid
  tv      screen on a stand
  mobile  handset with reception arcs
  phone   desk telephone

The same glyph now appears wherever a product family is referenced, so the
fiber mark on a plan card and the fiber column in the inclusions table are
visibly the same thing.

### [ADD] Three feature glyphs
`gauge` (speed), `gift` (free offer) and `layers` (tier) added to
`FeatureIcon`, for the hero's supporting promotions.

### [CHANGE] Icons placed on the cards that had no visual anchor
- Plan cards (cable, bundles, mobile) — service badge beside the plan
  name; header restructured to a flex row to hold it. 13 cards.
- Split panels (fiber, home phone) — 48px service badge above the eyebrow.
- Live TV tier cards — 36px badge above the eyebrow. 5 cards.
- Hero supporting promotions — icon in a bordered tile, card restructured
  to icon-beside-text. 3 cards.
- Inclusions table — service badge in each column header, and in the
  mobile card stack, so the columns are identifiable without reading.

Each badge inherits its surface's treatment (sky on glass over the dark
sections, electric on ice over the light ones) and picks up the card's
hover state.

### [NOTE] Build
A first `npm run build` after clearing `.next` failed in `next/font`
fetching the Google Fonts stylesheet — a network timeout, not a code
fault. The retry succeeded. Worth knowing because clearing `.next` drops
the cached font files, so the next build needs network.


## v1.6.0 — Static HTML export

### [BUILD] output: 'export'
`npm run build` now emits a folder of plain HTML/CSS/JS in `out/` that runs
on any static host with no Node process. The client bundle still ships, so
every animation behaves identically: Lenis smooth scrolling, the aurora
fields, the promo ring sweep and glow, the panel sheen and levitation, the
light motes, the panning headline gradient, 3D card tilt, magnetic buttons,
clip-path section wipes, staggered reveals and the CSS marquee.

`trailingSlash: true` so each route is its own directory with an
index.html, which is what shared hosting and bucket-style hosts expect.

Output:
  out/index.html
  out/legal/<8 policies>/index.html
  out/404.html
  out/images/         9 webp
  out/_next/          22 js chunks, 1 css, 11 woff2

### [CHANGE] images.unoptimized
The Next.js image optimizer is a server feature and cannot run on a static
host, so originals are served directly. This turned out to be an
improvement rather than a compromise: the hero now ships its full
2560x1598 source and renders at a cover scale of 0.68 — downscaled, so
genuinely sharp. Under the dev optimizer it was still being upscaled 1.20x.

### [FIX] Static export shipped a visible "$0" price
`CountUp` initialised its state to zero, so the prerendered HTML contained
`$0` for the featured offer and only corrected itself once JavaScript
hydrated and the count finished. Anyone without JavaScript, any crawler,
and any visitor whose tab was backgrounded (requestAnimationFrame is
suspended there, stranding the count at its start value) would have seen
the wrong price. A wrong price is a worse failure than a missing
animation.

Rewritten so correctness leads:
  - state starts at the FINAL value, so the exported HTML carries the real
    price
  - the reset to zero runs in a layout effect, before paint, so the count
    still starts from zero on screen with no flash of the total
  - a guard timer force-sets the final value if the animation has not
    completed in time, which covers the suspended-rAF case
Verified in out/index.html: both the width-reservation span and the
visible span now read 50.

### [CHANGE] Originals moved out of public/
`public/images/_source/` and the two markdown briefs were being copied
into the export, taking it to 24MB and publishing the untouched PNGs.
Moved to `image-sources/` at the project root. Export is now 4.5MB
(992KB images, 2.5MB JS/fonts/CSS).

### [QA] Static export verified served from python -m http.server
  /                         200
  /legal/tcpa-policy/       200
  /legal/pci-dss/           200
  /images/hero-ridges-dusk.webp  200
  hero image      2560x1598, cover scale 0.68 (sharp)
  aurora blobs    24
  light motes     6
  promo ring      1      sheen sweep 1      accent-live 4
  svg icons       202
  FAQ accordion   click toggles aria-expanded true, panel mounts
  ZIP form        submits, live region reads back the entered ZIP
  Lenis           html.lenis present, smooth scrolling active


## v1.7.0 — Badge fix, mobile responsiveness, repository

### [FIX] "Most popular" badge overlapped the plan name
The badge was absolutely positioned at `right-6 top-6`, and once the
service-line icon was added to the header the title shifted right and ran
underneath it — visible on "500 Mbps Internet".

Moved into normal flow as a badge rail at the top of the card. The rail
keeps its 24px height on every card, popular or not, so the speed block,
price and CTA still line up across a row — the alignment fixed in v1.0.

### [FIX] Desktop call button was rendering on phones
`MagneticButton`'s base class list includes `inline-flex`. Passing
`className="hidden sm:inline-flex"` did not hide it: `hidden` and
`inline-flex` sit in the same CSS layer, so the winner is decided by
stylesheet order, not by attribute order. The full "Call (855) 555-0143"
button was therefore visible at 375px, pushing the header 16px past the
viewport and giving the whole page a horizontal scroll.

Wrapped in a `<div className="hidden sm:block">` instead. Measured after:
page scrollWidth 391 -> 375 at a 375px viewport, no overflow.

### [CHANGE] Mobile responsiveness pass
- Type scales: hero h1 2.5rem -> 2rem (2.3rem from 400px), section
  headings 2rem -> 1.7rem, offer price 3.5rem -> 2.9rem, split-panel plan
  names 1.9rem -> 1.55rem, footer CTA 1.7rem -> 1.45rem, header wordmark
  0.98rem -> 0.86rem, all stepping back up at `sm`.
- Spacing: section padding py-20 -> py-14, hero pt-14/pb-24 -> pt-10/pb-16,
  grid top margins mt-14 -> mt-10, card padding p-7/p-8 -> p-6.
- Footer call CTA goes full width below `sm`.
- Hero stat rail gutters tighten so three columns fit at 375px.

### [CHANGE] Hero scrim switches axis on phones
The scrim ran left-to-right, which works when the copy sits in the left
column. On phones the copy spans the full width, so the ends of every line
fell outside the shaded area. Below `sm` the scrim now runs top-to-bottom,
following where the copy actually is.

### [CHANGE] Aurora fields scale down on phones
A 38rem blob behind a 375px viewport floods the section with one flat
colour, and a 150px blur is disproportionately expensive to composite on a
phone GPU for an effect nobody can resolve at that size. Blobs drop to
16-20rem and the blur to 80px below `sm`.

### [BUILD] Repository initialised
`git init`, branch `main`, one commit of 56 files / 8954 insertions,
remote set to https://github.com/PavithraEbbali/blueridge.git.

`.gitignore` extended to cover `.vercel` and `*.tsbuildinfo`. Build output
(`.next`, `out`) is not committed; `image-sources/` is, since those are the
untouched originals behind every processed image.

The push itself could not be completed from here: Git Credential Manager
has nothing cached for github.com, and supplying credentials is not
something to do on the user's behalf. The commit is ready; the user runs
`git push -u origin main` and authenticates in their own terminal.

### [BUILD] Vercel readiness
`output: 'export'` is detected automatically by Vercel — framework preset
Next.js, build command `npm run build`, output directory `out`. No
environment variables, no backend, no API routes. README documents the
import steps.
